WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.243
Many systems contain legacy password hashes, custom formats, weak work factors, or uncertain storage history.

2
00:00:07.243 --> 00:00:09.836
Migration should start with inventory.

3
00:00:09.836 --> 00:00:21.501
Identify the hash formats in use, approximate algorithm age, salt behavior, work factor, affected account population, and which applications or services verify those passwords.

4
00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:26.305
Safe migration does not mean recovering plaintext passwords from hashes.

5
00:00:26.305 --> 00:00:36.903
Instead, a system may rehash on successful login, force reset for high-risk accounts, stage rollout by account group, or move users through a planned recovery flow.

6
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:46.586
ASP. NET Core Identity-style versioned hashing supports the idea that older stored hashes can be upgraded when the user authenticates successfully.

7
00:00:46.586 --> 00:00:55.354
Communication matters. Users should receive clear instructions when a reset is required, and support teams should know what to expect.

8
00:00:55.354 --> 00:01:04.427
High-risk accounts, administrators, inactive users, service accounts, and accounts with unusual access may require different handling.

9
00:01:04.427 --> 00:01:09.383
Exceptions should be documented so they do not become permanent blind spots.

10
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:23.184
Suspected compromise response should include containment, evidence preservation, disabling risky sessions, rotation of related secrets, forced resets where appropriate, user notification, and monitoring.

11
00:01:23.184 --> 00:01:32.486
Related secrets may include signing keys, database credentials, email provider credentials, data-protection keys, and integration tokens.

12
00:01:32.486 --> 00:01:39.729
Breach response should coordinate legal, compliance, security, support, and engineering responsibilities.

13
00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:45.829
The team should document decisions, approvals, exceptions, user impact, and follow-up work.

14
00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:54.445
Password handling is not finished when the hash format changes; it is finished when the lifecycle and response plan are maintainable.
